# PHASE 14 — LAUNDRY & LINEN MANAGEMENT SYSTEM

## Master Developer Implementation Prompt

You are a senior Laravel engineer and healthcare operations systems architect.

Implement Phase 14: Laundry & Linen Management System within the existing Hospital Management System.

This must be a production-oriented implementation integrated into the existing Laravel Modular Monolith.

Do not build an isolated laundry application or duplicate existing hospital infrastructure.

---

# 1. ARCHITECTURE REQUIREMENTS

Preserve the established architecture:

- Laravel Modular Monolith.
- `nwidart/laravel-modules`.
- Laravel Blade.
- Tailwind CSS.
- MySQL/MariaDB.
- Existing Foundation authentication and RBAC.
- Existing branch management.
- Existing AuditLogService.
- Existing Inventory module.
- Existing HospitalOperations module.
- Existing Finance module.
- Existing notification infrastructure.

Primary module:

`Modules/Laundry`

If a laundry or linen module already exists, inspect and extend it.

Do not introduce React, Next.js, Vue, NestJS, microservices or another backend.

The system must support multiple hospital branches while maintaining one organization-wide operational architecture.

# 2. DEPENDENCY AUDIT

Before implementation, inspect:

1. Foundation users, permissions and branch access.
2. HospitalOperations departments, wards and beds.
3. Inventory items, warehouses, stock ledger and equipment register.
4. Finance service pricing, invoices and payments.
5. Existing procurement and supplier management.
6. Existing maintenance and work-order functionality.
7. Existing notifications.
8. Existing reporting and export infrastructure.
9. Existing audit logging.
10. Existing module navigation and UI conventions.

Produce a dependency report showing what already exists, what will be reused and what gaps remain.

Do not create duplicate supplier, procurement, inventory, accounting, employee or equipment management systems.

# 3. MODULE OBJECTIVES

The Laundry & Linen Management System must manage the complete operational lifecycle of hospital linen and reusable textile items.

It should support:

- Linen catalogue management.
- Linen classification.
- Linen ownership and department allocation.
- Linen stock and circulation tracking.
- Dirty linen collection.
- Collection requests.
- Dirty linen weighing and counting.
- Contaminated or special-handling linen identification.
- Sorting and segregation.
- Washing batch management.
- Disinfection process recording.
- Drying and ironing.
- Quality inspection.
- Rejected linen management.
- Clean linen storage.
- Distribution to wards and departments.
- Linen returns.
- Lost and damaged linen.
- Linen condemnation and retirement.
- Laundry equipment monitoring.
- Consumables integration.
- Laundry operational expenses.
- Laundry dashboards and reports.

The module must distinguish between reusable linen circulation and consumable inventory.

# 4. LINEN CATALOGUE

Create a configurable linen catalogue.

Examples:

- Bed sheets.
- Pillowcases.
- Blankets.
- Patient gowns.
- Towels.
- Staff uniforms.
- Surgical gowns.
- Theatre drapes.
- Curtains.
- Reusable cleaning cloths.
- Other approved hospital textiles.

Capture:

- Linen item code.
- Item name.
- Category.
- Description.
- Unit of measurement.
- Standard size.
- Colour or identification code.
- Ownership type.
- Replacement value, if maintained by Finance.
- Expected useful life.
- Approved washing instructions.
- Special handling requirements.
- Active status.

Support configurable categories rather than hardcoding a fixed list.

Distinguish:

1. Individually tracked reusable items.
2. Bulk-counted reusable items.
3. Consumable textile products.

Do not track a reusable sheet as a new procurement stock receipt every time it returns from washing.

# 5. LINEN IDENTIFICATION AND TRACKING

Support different tracking methods:

- Unique barcode.
- QR code.
- RFID-ready identifier.
- Batch or bulk quantity tracking.

For individually tracked items, generate a unique identifier.

Example:

`LIN-2026-000001`

Requirements:

- Prevent duplicate identifiers.
- Support barcode and QR scanning through a browser-compatible scanning approach where available.
- Do not require RFID hardware unless it actually exists.
- Preserve item movement history.
- Track current operational status.
- Track current location.
- Support missing-item investigation.

Suggested item statuses:

- Available
- Issued
- In Use
- Awaiting Collection
- Collected
- In Sorting
- Washing
- Drying
- Ironing
- Quality Inspection
- Clean Storage
- Damaged
- Missing
- Quarantined
